  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=0.8\textwidth]{Table 2 - householdhabits.png}
  \caption{Household behavior changes impact on emissions}
  \label{fig:Figure 2}
\end{figure}

Dietz et al. \cite{Dietz2009} demonstrated that household-level behavioral changes, if widely adopted, could cut U.S. emissions by 123 MtC annually. However, concerns for behavioral plasticity - how likely people are to implement each action based on financial and social incentives - remain an important factor \cite{Dietz2009}. Actions such as installing programmable thermostats demonstrate high emissions reductions and adoption likelihood, whereas vehicle upgrades show reductions but low likelihood. More often than not, individuals pointed to curtailment rather than implementing new products for energy efficiency in their households \cite{Attari2010}.

Studies also delved into the impact of current energy labels on consumer behavior, which would impact household decisions to invest in more energy-efficient appliances. This is important since choosing to buy a product/model accounts for approximately 8 of the choices shown in Figure 2. The current EnergyStar and EnergyGuide labels were reasonably effective. The biggest factor on consumer behavior was the cost savings, although this decreased in influence if the consumer cared less about future savings \cite{Newell2014}. Label effectiveness also depends on design: the EU label, for instance, underperformed due to poor clarity and limited consumer trust \cite{Andor2020}. This shows us that while current labels provide some benefit, they are lacking many of the key factors necessary to capture consumers' attention.

When consumers were asked to guess the energy savings of products, they undervalued "energy use and savings by a factor of 2.8 on average, with small overestimates for low-energy activities and large underestimates for high-energy activities," so energy labels are indeed helpful \cite{Attari2010}. A study by Allcott, Hunt, and Dmitry Taubinsky found that willingness to pay increased by \$2.30 for compact fluorescent lights in particular, and market share increased by 12\% when the benefits were displayed. They proposed that this would extend to most other household appliances. It is important to note that once the statistics on cost savings were unclear, the impact of all information dropped significantly \cite{Allcott2015}.

\section{Behavioral Economics}

Even with access to extensive information, consumers often fail to make optimal decisions. Anja Kollmuss and Julian Agyeman proposed a generalized model that incorporates personal values, knowledge, emotional involvement, and external factors like institutional support and social norms. They noted that they were not claiming that their model was fully sufficient for determining an individual's behaviors, nor did they claim that such a model was even feasible to create. Instead, the model serves as a base point to investigate what might cause homeowners to make their current, intuitive decisions.  

Kollmuss and Agyeman based it off of previous works, including Stern and associates' initial VBN theory \cite{Stern1999}, which states that the customers' decisions were influenced by their values, beliefs about environmental threats, and their ability to mitigate them, along with personal norms. Most widely accepted models and papers accepted the same general reasoning behind individual behavior, which applies to choices such as biking or walking instead of driving \cite{Steg2005, RezaeiMoghaddam2020, Wang2023, Lind2015}. While these frameworks offer a theoretical basis for understanding sustainable behavior, every study emphasizes that no single model can fully predict individual action, especially at the household level.


A major challenge with implementing these frameworks is the extra work required from users; although the benefits may be clear, some groups may not desire a switch in their routine, and having to wait for and consult a generated framework constantly. The decisions that the frameworks would be used for, such as purchasing a specific product or adjusting the thermostat, are often made using heuristics.

Heuristics are useful in everyday life, but under complex or high-stakes situations, they can lead to errors; multiple studies have found that under time pressure, people default to "satisficing" (choosing the first option that meets minimum criteria) or using dominant past behaviors, even if they're not ideal \cite{Klapproth2008}. Additionally, when these time constraints are combined with information overload, decision errors increase by approximately 20\% more errors in judgement \cite{Hahn1992}.  Since energy-use decisions often occur in high-distraction environments, tools that rely on sustained attention or complex input formats may fail to influence user behavior effectively. If people are willing to wait extra time, studies show that they are likely to make better decisions, which can be improved further with frameworks \cite{Nussbaum2013}.

Lerlerc and his associates found that time is treated as a decision-making resource, especially with heuristics. Consequently, the relationship between desirability and feasibility switches as the event approaches; a user may express willingness to use the models beforehand, but at the time of execution, many will refuse to stray from their routine \cite{Ariely2001} \cite{Leclerc1995}. This phenomenon reflects temporal discounting, which refers to the decrease in value for rewards based on time. Discounting behavior becomes more evident with shorter time frames: preferring $100 today, over $110 tomorrow, but $110 in a year over $100 in 11 months \cite{Ventre2022}. The problem with relying on a discount rate is that people are much more willing to wait for a concrete reward, but the benefits of reducing energy consumption and carbon output are often abstract. As a result, even tools that promise future benefits may be ignored if those benefits are not immediate, concrete, or personally meaningful. 

Despite these challenges, behavior can shift once routines are established. Habit strength plays a major role in whether sustainable actions persist over time. If a habit is solidified, the likelihood of continuation increases by up to a factor of three, and as stated by Benjamin Gardener and associates, reliance on intention and motivation decreases proportionally \cite{Mergelsberg2020} \cite{Ariely2001} \cite{Gardner2020}. 

These behavioral tendencies may negatively impact adoption of decision frameworks. In order to counteract the likelihood of participants and future users from abandoning the frameworks, we will need incentives, which could include moral and monetary incentives, either through direct rewards or demonstrated cost savings. Tools must minimize friction, provide immediate feedback, and reduce the cognitive and emotional cost of participation. Without these considerations, even the best frameworks will struggle to influence real-world household behavior.

\section{AI and Optimization Tools}

AI is increasingly being used to create decision frameworks such as MCDAs. It has shown promise in the Marketing and Medical industries \cite{Jackson2024} \cite{HuangRust2020} \cite{Simon2024}. Several studies have implemented frameworks, such as LCAs, MCDAs, MCDMs, Pareto Analysis, etc. successfully in environmental optimization decision making at a corporate or larger scale. LCA, for example, is frequently used in residential construction and can significantly impact long-term energy usage. It provides "a systematic analysis of environmental impact over the course of the entire life cycle of a product, material, process, or other measurable activity" \cite{Whitebell2020}. Although it hasn't been employed to make decisions on a large, coordinated scale for ordinary individuals, it is one of the most efficient ways to create frameworks currently.

There is one substantial problem: constructing a framework typically requires extensive knowledge of MCDA construction methods (depending on the situation, certain ones often perform better than others), rubric design, and knowledge of the decision at hand. To remedy this, Huang and Burgherr created a calculator that assesses the situation and the criterion, and is able to evaluate the result \cite{HuangBurgherr2024}. Although much more efficient than before, it still requires users to understand how to diagnose a situation's problems and be able to format them appropriately. As a result, they remain inaccessible to the average household. Bridging the gap with AI will solve both the short-term and long-term psychological costs addressed in the previous section.  
    
AI techniques have been integrated into Multi-Criteria Decision Making (MCDM) in supply chain manufacturing industries \cite{Belhadi2021}. These include the following: fuzzy systems, where the AI accounts for uncertainty and degrees of truth in order to mimic human reasoning; Wavelet Neural Networks, which can represent nonlinearities \cite{Alexandridis2013};  and Evaluation based on Distance from Average Solution, which "...is a method of Multiple-Criteria Decision-Making that determines the best alternative by calculating the distance between each solution and the average solution" \cite{Ullah2023}. These techniques have been successfully used across industries, and AI's ability to manage large datasets suggests it could scale down to personal decision-making as well.

