Modern systems also have a general knowledge of the energy use and carbon footprint of most products, among other metrics. AI has the ability to deduct these statistics from its own information, and also search the internet for useful information. However, AI has not been rigorously tested for individual-level decisions using longitudinal performance data. Additionally, AI systems tend to create potential biases by assuming pieces of knowledge, such as assuming the same climate patterns or energy sources apply across all locations \cite{Galaz2021} \cite{Nishant2020}. These challenges may lead the AI to assume statistics based on geography, since AI is used much more in certain regions. Figure 4 \cite{Galaz2021} illustrates how geographic concentration in training data can lead to skewed AI performance, particularly when the model is unaware of the specific region in which it is being applied. These can be prevented, but only if the model knows to search for data on its current location.

